# Reference verification, batch 4: s020-s027 (Nov 2026)
upd("n392", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* IX q. 1 (Badius 1518 via SCTA), ‘Vtrum omnis relatio inter deum & creaturam sit quia ipsa creatura refertur ad deum’, is the locus. IX q. 2 is on the plurality of ideas and is related only. Verified for IX q. 1.]", status="verified")
upd("n393", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: *Quodl.* III q. 9 (‘Vtrum sit ponere aliquam essentiam per indifferentiam se habentem ad esse & ad non esse’: the creature is *ens aliquod per essentiam* by its respect to the divine exemplar) and V q. 2 (‘dicitur res a ratitudine. Et haec res est super quam fundatur prima ratio praedicamenti’) fit. IV q. 7 (the intellect’s self-knowledge without species) and II q. 3 (on Christ’s flesh) do not bear on *ratitudo*. Verified for III q. 9 and V q. 2; the other two references look wrong.]", status="verified")
upd("n406", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: *Quodl.* IV q. 8 asks whether the blessed who sees God forms a *verbum* of God in himself. The denial of the species is closer in IV q. 7 (whether the intellect knows itself *absque omni specie*) and in V q. 14 (‘hoc non potest esse species intelligibilis impressa intellectui’). Verified, with IV q. 7 the closer locus.]", status="verified")
upd("n411", append="[Checked: Aug. *De Trin.* XI 9.16 (augustinus.it): ‘quattuor species reperiuntur quasi gradatim natae altera ex altera’ (in the body, in the sense, in memory, *in acie cogitantis*). Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n412", append="[Checked: Aug. *De Gen. ad litt.* IV (augustinus.it) develops the *vespera*/*mane* of angelic knowledge from c. 22 n. 39 (‘post vesperam fiat mane, cum post cognitionem suae propriae naturae … refert se ad laudandam lucem’) through c. 35. The *vespertina cognitio* is named at c. 30 n. 47. Verified for IV 22–35.]", status="verified")
upd("n418", append="[Checked: the Wadding/Vivès text (SCTA) of *Ord.* I d. 3 p. 3 also has only four questions, so the extra question isn’t there either. The conservation of the species is argued only at the close of p. 3 q. 1 (‘nisi ponatur eam posse conservare speciem intelligibilem praeter actum’). The incunables were not consulted. Still open.]")
upd("n420", append="[Checked: no searchable Latin Averroes *In De anima* or Avicenna *De anima* was consulted, and the phrase *arca specierum* was not found. Still open.]")
upd("n425", append="[Checked: Wadding/Vivès d. 3 p. 3 has no such question either. *Quodl.* q. 13 (Vivès XXV, archive.org operaomnia25duns) does treat operation as an absolute quality (‘nullo modo erit actio de genere Actionis’). The source of Gorris’s question is still open.]")
upd("n429", append="[Checked: Scotus, *Quodl.* q. 13 (Vivès XXV, archive.org operaomnia25duns, c. pp. 575–581) holds that intellection is a *qualitas absoluta* and ‘nullo modo erit actio de genere Actionis’. The marginal cross-reference there reads ‘Operatio est qualitas, de quo late 1, d. 3. q. 6’ (Wadding numbering). The Scotus locus is verified. Whether Gorris’s *quidam* means Scotus himself is still interpretation.]", status="verified")
upd("n434", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: *Quodl.* IV q. 7 (self-knowledge without species) plausibly bears on the soul’s activity. V q. 5 (whether God causes the damnation of the wicked) does not fit, and may be a slip. The Vatican’s XIII q. 8 (‘Vtrum intellectus agens pertineat ad imaginem’) and V q. 14 fit better. Partly confirmed.]", status="uncertain")
upd("n435", append="[Checked: Godfrey, *Quodl.* VI q. 7 (SCTA), ‘Utrum voluntas potest se movere’, argues against self-motion in general and discusses the intellect–phantasm case, so Gorris’s ‘VI q. 7’ is defensible and need not be a slip. VI q. 15 (whether the agent intellect belongs to the superior reason) has ‘per abstractionem speciei … mediante phantasmate’. VIII q. 2 (Hoffmans, Philosophes Belges IV, archive.org: whether a subject can be the immediate effective principle of its own accident) and XIII q. 3 (Philosophes Belges V: whether a created substance can by itself be the immediate principle of an operation) fit. IX q. 19 was not available online. Verified, except IX q. 19.]", status="verified")
upd("n436", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: *Quodl.* V q. 14 fits. The intellect is *quasi actiue … quasi passiue*, and the agent intellect abstracts and moves the possible intellect to simple apprehension. III q. 6 (whether the vegetative and sensitive souls in Christ were educed from matter) does not fit, so Gorris’s ‘III q. 6’ for the retracting position is doubtful. Verified for V q. 14.]", status="verified")
upd("n439", append="[Checked: the lemma *Sexta opinio, quae redit in idem* stands in the Wadding/Vivès text of p. 3 q. 2 (SCTA). Giles’s *Quodlibeta* III qq. 12–14 are not available online. Still open.]")
upd("n440", append="[Checked: the harmonization (‘Iste tamen duae opiniones, scilicet tertia et quarta … concordantur ad invicem’) is in the Wadding/Vivès text of p. 3 q. 2 (SCTA). Henry’s *Quodl.* IV q. 8 (Badius 1518 via SCTA) is on whether the blessed forms a *verbum*. I could not find the *occursus* doctrine there or anywhere in the Quodlibeta (*occursu* occurs only in XV q. 2). The reference is doubtful.]", status="uncertain")
upd("n441", text="The lemma is in the Wadding/Vivès text of *Ord.* I d. 3 p. 3 q. 2 (SCTA), in Scotus’s argument against the harmonization of the third and fourth opinions: ‘sicut ultimus gradus habitus generatur ex ultimo actu, ita primus ex primo … Hoc confirmatur secundum opinionem illius Doctoris qui exponitur, quia ponit istam inchoationem esse essentiam habitus totam. Quod ulterius dicitur de occursu illo…’. So it is not an extra passage; the earlier note was wrong to say so. Vatican paragraph numbers were not checked. Gorris’s Henry reference, *Quodl.* V q. 19 (Badius 1518 via SCTA), opens the questions on infused habits (‘vtrum habitus infusus possit augeri’). The subject (degrees of a habit) is related, but the thesis that the *inchoatio* is the whole essence of the habit was not found in the short SCTA text.", status="uncertain")
upd("n447", append="[Checked: no searchable text of Ockham’s *Reportatio* II (OTh V) or Rimini’s *In II Sent.* was consulted. Still open.]")
upd("n452", append="[Checked: no searchable text of Antonius Andreas or Meyronnes was found. Still open.]")
upd("n461", append="[Checked: no external source for the first-person passage was found. Still open.]")
upd("n463", append="[Checked: the formula was not found in the Wadding/Vivès *Ordinatio* I d. 3 (SCTA), and no later Scotist texts were consulted. Still open.]")
upd("n470", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: *Quodl.* VI q. 1 (‘Utrum sint tres in divinis personae, nec plures nec pauciores’: two emanations by intellect and will) and VI q. 2 (whether the production of the persons is presupposed to that of creatures) bear on the Father’s fecundity and the order of the emanations. They fit Gorris’s reference in general. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n473", append="[Checked: Thomas, *ST* I q. 93 a. 6 co. (Corpus Thomisticum): the image ‘repraesentat secundum similitudinem speciei’, and the vestige ‘ad speciei similitudinem non pertingit’ (e.g. the *vestigia* left by moving animals). Verified. Gorris’s formula still differs from Thomas’s, as noted above. Also confirmed: *ST* I q. 85 a. 2 co. (the opinion that the species *is* what is understood) for the fifth opinion at s024, and *ST* I q. 87 a. 1.]", status="verified")
